1
00:00:00,283 --> 00:00:02,340
Fresh produce has a deadline.

2
00:00:02,350 --> 00:00:03,748
One late route can turn a valuable

3
00:00:03,758 --> 00:00:05,348
batch into waste.

4
00:00:05,358 --> 00:00:07,807
FreshRoute LastWindow helps distributors make the next

5
00:00:07,817 --> 00:00:09,250
decision before that window closes.

6
00:00:11,283 --> 00:00:12,878
Our problem is not simply finding the

7
00:00:12,888 --> 00:00:14,232
shortest route.

8
00:00:14,242 --> 00:00:17,151
Dispatchers must coordinate shelf life, receiving hours,

9
00:00:17,161 --> 00:00:20,973
vehicle capacity, demand, permitted discounts, and costs.

10
00:00:20,983 --> 00:00:23,855
LastWindow brings these constraints into one workspace,

11
00:00:23,865 --> 00:00:26,003
so operational decisions can be examined together.

12
00:00:27,283 --> 00:00:29,584
Here is the working product: six batches,

13
00:00:29,594 --> 00:00:32,127
three vehicles, and three destinations.

14
00:00:32,137 --> 00:00:34,290
Generate Plan turns available inventory into a

15
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:36,267
proposed dispatch schedule.

16
00:00:36,277 --> 00:00:38,587
The dashboard connects the route network, shelf-life

17
00:00:38,597 --> 00:00:42,516
watchlist, forecast contribution, and independent validation.

18
00:00:42,526 --> 00:00:44,126
Every number comes from the scenario and

19
00:00:44,136 --> 00:00:45,015
its generated plan.

20
00:00:46,282 --> 00:00:49,746
Underneath, an OR-Tools mixed-integer model chooses quantities,

21
00:00:49,756 --> 00:00:52,995
routes, departure times, vehicles, and allowed price

22
00:00:53,005 --> 00:00:54,108
tiers.

23
00:00:54,118 --> 00:00:56,924
It accounts for capacity, receiving windows, freshness,

24
00:00:56,934 --> 00:00:58,242
and costs.

25
00:00:58,252 --> 00:01:00,112
A separate validator checks the result before

26
00:01:00,122 --> 00:01:01,286
commitment.

27
00:01:01,296 --> 00:01:03,345
Optimality is claimed only within the generated

28
00:01:03,355 --> 00:01:04,095
candidate set.

29
00:01:05,283 --> 00:01:07,196
Our key idea is the last window:

30
00:01:07,206 --> 00:01:09,023
the latest feasible departure before a batch

31
00:01:09,033 --> 00:01:10,776
loses its opportunity.

32
00:01:10,786 --> 00:01:12,438
Dispatch slack shows how much room is

33
00:01:12,448 --> 00:01:13,486
left.

34
00:01:13,496 --> 00:01:16,130
A decision receipt explains each batch's allocations,

35
00:01:16,140 --> 00:01:19,147
constraints, and price-tier comparisons, so the operator

36
00:01:19,157 --> 00:01:21,360
can understand the recommendation instead of following

37
00:01:21,370 --> 00:01:21,987
a black box.

38
00:01:23,283 --> 00:01:25,844
Now a working workflow: reserve the plan,

39
00:01:25,854 --> 00:01:28,111
then record a twenty-minute route delay.

40
00:01:28,121 --> 00:01:30,448
Existing stock and vehicles remain locked, and

41
00:01:30,458 --> 00:01:32,828
conflicting reservations are flagged.

42
00:01:32,838 --> 00:01:34,198
An unstarted trip can move to a

43
00:01:34,208 --> 00:01:36,340
feasible later slot, or be released for

44
00:01:36,350 --> 00:01:38,086
its cancellation fee.

45
00:01:38,096 --> 00:01:41,027
Replanning uses the recovered availability, while historical

46
00:01:41,038 --> 00:01:42,546
plans retain their original snapshots.

47
00:01:44,283 --> 00:01:47,173
LastWindow also compares bounded policy candidates across

48
00:01:47,183 --> 00:01:50,957
sampled travel, shelf-life, and demand conditions.

49
00:01:50,967 --> 00:01:52,982
It can rank batches by estimated inspection

50
00:01:52,992 --> 00:01:54,094
value.

51
00:01:54,104 --> 00:01:56,898
Simulating an inspection consumes time, records its

52
00:01:56,908 --> 00:01:59,544
cost, updates the estimate, and preserves the

53
00:01:59,554 --> 00:02:00,907
observation.

54
00:02:00,917 --> 00:02:02,715
Inspect only when the information is worth

55
00:02:02,725 --> 00:02:03,683
its operational cost.

56
00:02:05,283 --> 00:02:07,465
The evaluation view replays stored plans under

57
00:02:07,475 --> 00:02:09,536
seeded simulation worlds.

58
00:02:09,546 --> 00:02:14,690
We compare illustrative first-in-first-out, earliest-expiry-first, nominal optimization,

59
00:02:14,700 --> 00:02:17,811
and uncertainty-aware selection under shared conditions.

60
00:02:17,821 --> 00:02:20,940
Sales, spoilage, carryover, service failures, and net

61
00:02:20,950 --> 00:02:23,332
contribution are reported separately.

62
00:02:23,342 --> 00:02:25,704
These are synthetic results, not field measurements.

63
00:02:27,283 --> 00:02:29,982
React and TypeScript power the interface.

64
00:02:29,992 --> 00:02:33,744
FastAPI, OR-Tools with SCIP, and SQLite support

65
00:02:33,754 --> 00:02:35,961
planning and saved snapshots.

66
00:02:35,971 --> 00:02:38,573
The build includes cancellable jobs, revision checks,

67
00:02:38,583 --> 00:02:40,732
and optional password access.

68
00:02:40,742 --> 00:02:44,102
Seventy-three backend tests, nine frontend tests, and

69
00:02:44,112 --> 00:02:45,696
seven browser workflows have passed.

70
00:02:47,283 --> 00:02:50,665
LastWindow makes perishable distribution explainable, time-aware, and

71
00:02:50,675 --> 00:02:51,952
recoverable.

72
00:02:51,962 --> 00:02:53,211
The next step is a pilot with

73
00:02:53,221 --> 00:02:54,986
real operational data.

74
00:02:54,996 --> 00:02:57,333
Better decisions, before freshness runs out.
